Producing Fluids in a Fixed Sequence
Produce Fluids in a Fixed Sequence
In this example we model a simple production line in which a FluidSource produces three materials in a
fixed sequence.
The FluidSource 
  produces the materials in the sequence and with the amount in the order in which you
enter them into the sequence table.
The materials then flow through Pipes to a PatchMatrix 
 , which distributes the materials via three Pipes
to the Tanks 
  storing the individual materials. We define how the PatchMatrix fills the Tanks in a Method,
which is called by the init method.
In our example we will demonstrate how to:
• Configure FluidSource, MaterialsTable, and SequenceTable
• Configure the Tanks Receiving the Materials
• Define How the PatchMatrix Fills the Tanks
• Run the Simulation to View the Results
We also inserted a Pipe between the FluidSource and the PatchMatrix and three Pipes from the
PatchMatrix to the three Tanks. Remember that you have to establish connections between Pipes and
fluid objects with Connectors.
The finished simulation model looks like this:
Producing Fluids in a Fixed Sequence
10-408 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Simulate Free-flowing Materials and Fluids
Configure FluidSource, MaterialsTable, and SequenceTable
After we inserted the objects into our simulation model, we first configure the FluidSource that produces
the fluid materials.
• As we want it to produce them in a fixed sequence, we select Material Selection > Sequence on the tab
Attributes.
The FluidSource produces the materials in the sequence and with the amount in the order in which you
enter them into the Sequence Table.
The setting Sequence processes the sequence only once, not repeatedly. When the sequence is
processed once, the FluidSource does not produce any additional materials.
Configure FluidSource, MaterialsTable, and SequenceTable
Plant Simulation Help 10-409
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• We then entered the materials MatA, MatB, and MatC into the MaterialsTable. To be able to distinguish
them when they flow through the Pipes, we assigned them different colors.
• We finally entered the sequence in which the FluidSource produces the materials and the required
amount of the Material into the SequenceTable. The FluidSource first produces 1000 liters of MatB,
then 1500 liters of MatC, and finally 800 liters of MatA.
Back to Simulate Free-flowing Materials and Fluids
Configure FluidSource, MaterialsTable, and SequenceTable
10-410 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Configure the Tanks Receiving the Materials
Next we inserted a Tank each for the three materials.
As we use the same settings for each of them, namely an Outflow rate of 1 liter per second and a Volume
of 3000 liters, we enter them into the class of the Tank:
Back to Simulate Free-flowing Materials and Fluids
Define How the PatchMatrix Fills the Tanks
When we connect the PatchMatrix with the incoming and outgoing Pipes, it enters them into the
Connections table.
Configure the Tanks Receiving the Materials
Plant Simulation Help 10-411
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Normally you set which outgoing Pipes the PatchMatrix connects with the incoming Pipes by selecting the
respective check box(es).
Our modeling situation is different though. Our FluidSource produces different amounts of the three
materials in a fixed sequence. The PatchMatrix thus has to connect the incoming Pipe with the respective
outgoing Pipe to fill the three Tanks with the correct material.
We programmed that in our method named fillTanks.
var currMaterial = "" 
waituntil FluidSource.CurrentMaterial /= currMaterial
currMaterial = FluidSource.CurrentMaterial
PatchMatrix.setConnections(1, 1, true)    // lets the first material flow 
into the outgoing Pipe1
waituntil FluidSource.CurrentMaterial /= currMaterial
currMaterial = FluidSource.CurrentMaterial
PatchMatrix.setConnections(1, 1, false)   // closes Pipe1
PatchMatrix.setConnections(1, 2, true)    // lets the second material flow 
into the outgoing Pipe2
waituntil FluidSource.CurrentMaterial /= currMaterial
currMaterial = FluidSource.CurrentMaterial
PatchMatrix.setConnections(1, 2, false)   // closes Pipe2
PatchMatrix.setConnections(1, 3, true)    // lets the third material flow 
into the outgoing Pipe3
The method fillTanks is called in our init method.
PatchMatrix.resetConnections
fillTanks
We also reset the connections of the PatchMatrix in our reset method.
PatchMatrix.resetConnections
Back to Simulate Free-flowing Materials and Fluids
Run the Simulation to View the Results
When we run the simulation, we can watch the FluidSource producing the materials in the sequence
specified in the SequenceTable one after the other. The color of the Pipe shows which material flows. The
PatchMatrix then sends the arriving material on to the correct Tank by opening the respective Pipe leading
to that Tank.
To better see the results, we set a real-time factor of 42 in the EventController.
Run the Simulation to View the Results
10-412 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

First the FluidSource produces 1000 liters of MatB, designated by the green color we specified in the
MaterialsTable, and passes the material on to the PatchMatrix.
It opens Pipe1 to let MatB flow into the TankMatB.
Next flows MatC and finally MatA. At the end of the simulation all Tanks are filled with the specified
amount of the specified material.
Run the Simulation to View the Results
